{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"trilateral-prosperous-cusimanse","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nA plan that passes `review-plan` can still enumerate a file set that is **smaller than\nthe one its own work compels**. The gate certifies the plan's reasoning; it does not\nsweep the tree to confirm the enumeration is closed under the change's own\nconsequences. So a certified plan can carry an acceptance criterion it is structurally\nincapable of satisfying, and nobody learns until implementation.\n\nThe failure is quiet in the worst way: the plan is certified, the author trusts the\nenumeration, and the gap surfaces either as a self-contradicting AC at close time or\nas an implementation that silently exceeds its reviewed scope.\n\n## Observation (measured)\n\nThree instances, all verified against the tree rather than recalled:\n\n**1. `dc42` — a plan whose own AC its file list could not satisfy.**\nAC1 required `grep -r fidelity_spot_eval src docs` to return no hits. The plan's file\nenumeration omitted `docs/research/task-decomposition-sota-2026.md`, which cited\n`fidelity_spot_eval` at line 167 as a working batch-rerun seam. With that hit present\nAC1 could never pass, so the plan as certified was unsatisfiable. The landed commit\n`e2ed08b343a` does touch that file, confirming the omission was real and compelled.\n\n**2. `dc42` — a module-wide deletion planned as a single-test deletion.**\nThe plan named one test in `tests/unit/test_plan_review_prerequisites_continuation_heldout.py`.\nThe module-level import is shared by all 4 tests and 3 of them call into the deleted\nmodule, so the whole file had to go. `e2ed08b343a` deletes it wholesale: 127 deletions,\n0 insertions.\n\n**3. `25a4` — an AC enumerating 5 paths where 6 are compelled.**\nAC8 lists 5 paths. `tests/unit/test_gen_env_registry.py:67`\n(`test_check_mode_clean_against_committed_tree`) asserts `gen.main([\"--check\"]) == 0`,\nwhich requires the committed `docs/env-vars.md` to match generator output. Any change\nto the env-var reads therefore compels regenerating that doc, making the true set 6.\nThe landed commit `e096a33d3b7` touches 6 paths. The AC is unsatisfiable as written and\nis currently blocking that ticket's close.\n\nA wider pattern was suspected across more tickets. That broader count is **unverified**\nand is deliberately not claimed here.\n\nWhat distinguishes all three from ordinary plan drift: the omitted file is not\nincidental, it is *forced* by the change the plan already describes. A per-symbol sweep\nof the whole tree finds them; sampling citations does not. In instance 1 that distinction\nis exactly what was observed — sampling passed, the sweep found the hit.\n\n## Approach (direction, not a committed design)\n\nGive plan review a mechanical closure check to complement its reasoning: for the symbols\na plan says it removes or renames, sweep the tree and compare the resulting hit set\nagainst the plan's enumerated paths, reporting any path that carries a hit but is not\nenumerated. The same sweep catches a test module whose surviving tests depend on a\ndeleted import.\n\nWhether this belongs as a new blocking criterion, an advisory finding, or a\npreflight the author runs before requesting review is for the implementer to determine.\nAn advisory finding is the cheaper first step and does not risk blocking correct plans\non an imprecise sweep.\n\n## Scope\n\n- The check's home (criterion, advisory finding, or author-run preflight) is open.\n- Tests covering a plan whose enumeration is closed and one whose enumeration is not.\n- Documentation of the check wherever plan-review criteria are described.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: Given a plan that names a symbol for removal and enumerates a file set that\n      omits a tree path carrying that symbol, the review output names the omitted path.\n- [ ] AC2: Given a plan whose enumeration already covers every path carrying the named\n      symbols, no such finding is emitted.\n- [ ] AC3: Given a plan that deletes a module and names only one test in a test file\n      whose module-level import binds that module, the review output identifies the\n      remaining tests in that file as affected.\n- [ ] AC4: The check is documented alongside the other plan-review criteria, and\n      `rebar explain` resolves it if it is registered as a criterion.\n- [ ] AC5: New tests cover AC1, AC2 and AC3 and are red before the change.\n- [ ] AC6: `make verify` exits 0, and the Gerrit change carries `LLM-Review +1` and\n      `Verified +1`.","id":"c9c2-c569-f5cb-426f","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Certified plans enumerate file sets their own work compels them to exceed"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793420932060265373,"uuid":"b665d58a-20c2-4cce-bf63-24056a4dfd65"}